markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Objects for Real-world Data

Now that you have explored organizing and updating lists with array methods, it's time for the next step: representing more complex things in their programs. 

Real-world items usually aren’t just one piece of information—they have many details. That’s where **objects** come in.

<img src="https://images.code.org/125d7ab017a32085476933e0821b04f1-image-1765988268219.png" alt="backpack labeled as object with properties of type color size" width="200px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 40px 40px 40px 40px;">

## What is an object?

An object is a set of related information grouped under one name.

It lets you represent a real-world thing (like a backpack or dessert) in code.

We can explain the backpack with descriptive properties.

- **type**
- **color**
- **size**


<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

## Properties 

<div style="display: flex; gap: 24px; align-items: flex-start; margin: 24px 0;">

  <!-- Left: Definition (2/3) -->
  <div style="flex: 2;">
    <p style="margin: 4px 0; font-size: 14pt;">
      <strong>A property</strong> is a named piece of information inside an object.
    </p><br>
    <p style="margin: 4px 0; font-size: 14pt;">
      It tells you a specific fact about that object that your program can read or update.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Right: Backpack Properties (1/3, gray box) -->
  <div style="
    flex: 1;
    background-color: #f5f5f5;
    border-radius: 8px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);
    padding: 16px 20px;
  ">
    <p>
    <b>Backpack Properties</b>
    </p>
    <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 0;">
      <li style="font-size: 14pt;margin: 4px 0;"><strong>type:</strong> canvas</li>
      <li style="font-size: 14pt;margin: 4px 0;"><strong>color:</strong> blue</li>
      <li style="font-size: 14pt;margin: 4px 0;"><strong>size:</strong> 30</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

</div>


<div style="clear:both;"></div>



---

## Quick Check: Build an Object

Choose one object below. Before clicking, think of 3 properties it might have in a program. Then click to compare your ideas with one possible example.

<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 1fr);gap:16px;margin:24px 0;">

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;text-align:center;">
      🍕 Pizza
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;">
      <strong>Possible properties:</strong><br>
      size → string<br>
      toppings → string<br>
      isDelivery → boolean
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;text-align:center;">
      🎮 Video Game
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;">
      <strong>Possible properties:</strong><br>
      title → string<br>
      genre → string<br>
      isMultiplayer → boolean
    </div>
  </details>

  <details style="background:#F5F7F8;border-radius:14px;padding:18px;box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-size:18px;font-weight:700;list-style:none;text-align:center;">
      🐶 Pet
    </summary>
    <div style="margin-top:14px;font-size:16px;line-height:1.6;">
      <strong>Possible properties:</strong><br>
      name → string<br>
      age → number<br>
      isAdopted → boolean
    </div>
  </details>

</div>

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5;border-radius:40px;color:#000000;width:95%;padding:20px 24px;margin:24px 0;">

<p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.5;margin:0;">
<strong>💡 Teacher Tip:</strong> Have students start with a real-world object, like a water bottle, backpack, or snack, and describe facts the program would need to know about it. This helps students distinguish between the object itself and its properties.
</p>

</div>

<p style="font-size:17px;line-height:1.5;">
In the next level, you will choose your own object idea and turn it into code.
</p>

MARKDOWN
